+# #158 · Qualify the GitHub merge fence
+
+This spike determines whether GitHub can enforce one merge path with
+the exact tree and Stop the Line ordering required by T21. The result
+is a reproducible capability receipt for one permitted merge method or
+a refusal that identifies the blocking condition. The spike carries
+3.5 implementation agent-hours, P1, and has no story predecessor.
+[#158 story](../story.md) · [Accepted #133 design §6](https://github.com/kburson/aitm/blob/2ff1b49ed3a3f91c8164d6c7b0e1dbe4c15cd3f9/.aitm/stories/001/133/design/test-impact-analysis-design.md#L394) · [Accepted T21](https://github.com/kburson/aitm/blob/dbe0d68b3e7b977c9fbf51e26d9ae8c6f629af84/.aitm/stories/001/133/plan/test-impact-analysis-implementation-plan.md#L507)
+
+## Isolation and authority
+
+Use an owner-authorized disposable GitHub repository with synthetic
+commits, an isolated target branch and separate fixture PRs. Pin the
+repository ID, owner type, visibility, plan and applicable rule
+capabilities. The owner provides or expressly authorizes the fixture,
+its rules administrator, a repository-scoped dedicated trusted merge
+actor, a separate reviewer and trusted check publisher, and ordinary
+writer and automation test identities. An administrator may configure
+the fixture. Their ability to edit protection is an administrative
+trust boundary and is distinct from their ordinary merge rights.
+Test any admin role that could merge without editing policy. The
+merge identity must have no rule administration and its credentials
+must be inaccessible to candidates and ordinary workflows. Keep the
+sandbox and evidence within its authorized scope. Production AITM
+protection, workflow, gate and source are outside the write set.
+
+The selected hypothesis is a GitHub App installation dedicated to the
+trusted merge driver, a target update restriction with the App as its
+sole PR-only exception, and a separate active ruleset with no bypass
+for required PR reviews, trusted-source checks and strict up-to-date
+checks. Protect against target deletion and force updates. Permit
+only a merge commit and disable auto-merge and merge queue on the
+candidate configuration. Start with the synchronous GitHub REST PR
+merge carrying `sha=H` and `merge_method=merge`. The selected rule
+combination, credential custody and host enforcement are hypotheses
+to measure. PR-only bypass must neither admit a direct ref update nor
+bypass the separate review and check requirements. Exclude generic
+write access to the trusted credential and automatic retries from
+the driver. [GitHub ruleset configuration](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/creating-rulesets-for-a-repository) · [Available rules](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/available-rules-for-rulesets) · [GitHub PR merge API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/pulls/pulls?apiVersion=2026-03-10#merge-a-pull-request)
+
+GitHub documents an expected PR head for REST and GraphQL merge, and
+the REST request contract exposes neither an expected base OID nor a
+stop version. Strict checks can strengthen the route but must be shown
+to bind the exact base and tested tree at mutation. Exclusive writer
+serialization is a possible equivalent base fence only if every
+target writer and outstanding provider operation is accounted for.
+The asynchronous endpoint can create deferred work and expose its
+status. A pending UUID, expiring lookup and queue receipt supply no
+stop barrier. Treat these APIs as alternate routes to block or
+account for. An atomic generic ref update is a distinct path that
+would need its own proof of the conditional PR merge and retained
+review/check contract. This spike probes the selected PR route and
+does not widen the design if it fails.
+[GraphQL merge input](https://docs.github.com/en/graphql/reference/pulls#mergepullrequestinput) · [Async merge results](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/pulls/pulls?apiVersion=2026-03-10#get-the-result-of-an-asynchronous-merge) · [GraphQL ref updates](https://docs.github.com/en/graphql/reference/git#updaterefs)
+
+## Evidence required at mutation
+
+For each reservation pin repository ID, full target ref, PR identity,
+base OID `B`, head OID `H`, selected method, protection snapshot,
+trusted check and review receipts, tested merge tree `T` and ordered
+authority version. Before marking the case green, verify that the
+fixture tested merge commit has exactly the ordered parents `B,H`.
+Use the host-generated test merge when available. On success verify
+the PR merge event or provider response and target ref identify the
+landed commit, its ordered parents are `B,H` and its tree OID equals
+`T`. Commit metadata may change the commit OID. A local calculation,
+green status alone, preflight check, or post-landing comparison alone
+cannot enforce the mutation precondition. The host control must
+prevent a stale or different tree from landing.
+
+Require host ordering proof for the effective stop and the target
+mutation. A stop observed between reservation and dispatch cancels
+the stale reservation before any merge request leaves the driver.
+Once a merge may have reached GitHub, record the stop and retain the
+pending intent durably, freeze all further merge mutations including
+repair and retries, and read back. The only resolved outcomes are an
+exact host commit demonstrably before the stop, which enters the
+incident baseline, or authoritative terminal evidence that the
+request cannot commit. A stop followed by a successful ordinary
+update falsifies the candidate. Keep unresolved state held when
+ordering or terminality is unknown. Request acceptance, transport
+timeout, elapsed time, an open PR, an unchanged branch, a local epoch,
+or unrelated clocks cannot decide the order. A late-arriving response
+can confirm a commit already proven before the stop. It cannot move
+the stop boundary retroactively. A rule edit, token revocation or
+status change can serve as a boundary only with host evidence that
+covers already in-flight requests.
+
+## Receipt and dependency disposition
+
+Retain a sanitized spike report and disposable rule evidence in
+`attachments/github-merge-fence-spike.md` and
+`attachments/github-merge-fence-rules.json` in the #158 story when
+authorized. The receipt records the exact configuration, provider
+contract, actor capabilities, request and event identifiers, tested
+and landed Git object identities, before/after provider readback,
+ordering evidence, and one disposition for every planned case.
+
+- **Qualified.** One named method satisfies exclusivity, review and
+  checks, exact head/base/tree at mutation, and stop ordering. Give
+  #159 the tested configuration, supported ordering and readback
+  contract, plus drift and hold conditions.
+- **Refused.** Authorized provider evidence shows an allowed bypass,
+  stale/wrong tree or post-stop commit, or establishes that a necessary
+  guarantee cannot be demonstrated within the tested host route.
+  Record the failing control and explicitly mark any remaining cases
+  unrun. This can satisfy the spike's evidence deliverable through
+  its own review and closure, while #159 and descendants stay held.
+  An unresolved live intent stays fenced and owned for reconciliation.
+- **Pickup held.** Missing sandbox authority, identity, access or
+  accepted leaf planning prevents execution. Record which grant or
+  capability is missing. This is separate from a demonstrated host
+  refusal. No qualification, successor release or completion claim
+  follows from setup being unavailable.
+
+#159 also requires #157's authority interface and its own review,
+planning and implementation authorization. A qualified receipt
+supplies only the #158 capability. Full PR validation stays in force.
